# Cache policy for Cloudflare Pages / Netlify.
#
# Fingerprinted assets under /assets/ get an aggressive year-long cache;
# their names change on every build so this is safe. index.html + the
# service worker stay no-cache so a redeploy actually reaches every user
# on next load — a cached SW that has gone bad is very hard to recover
# from otherwise.

# `immutable` was removed on 2026-08-18 after it turned a transient bad
# response into a week-long outage.
#
# The filenames are content-hashed, so a long max-age is still correct and
# is kept. But `immutable` additionally tells the browser not to revalidate
# even on an explicit reload. Combined with _redirects' `/* /index.html 200`
# catch-all — which returns HTML with a **200** rather than a 404 whenever a
# file is briefly unreachable — any browser that once cached that HTML under
# an asset URL served it for a year and never asked the server again. The
# page rendered blank on those machines only, which is why it looked random
# and why purging the CDN would not have reached them.
#
# Without `immutable` the cache still holds for a year in normal use, but a
# refresh can revalidate and recover. That escape hatch is worth far more
# than the handful of revalidation requests it costs.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/firebase-messaging-sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
